          "title": "1.3 Agricultural Systems: Subsistence, Commercial, Mixed Farming",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/20280060/pexels-photo-20280060.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&dpr=2&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RXNjGkJe7fY",
          "content": "Agricultural systems are ways in which farming is organized and practiced. They can be broadly classified based on their scale, purpose, inputs, outputs, and integration.\n\nSubsistence Farming (Traditional Farming):\n\nPrimary Purpose: To produce food and other agricultural products mainly for the consumption of the farmer and their family. Little or no surplus is produced for sale.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nSmall scale of operation (small landholdings).\n\nHigh reliance on manual labor and family labor; limited use of mechanization.\n\nLow capital investment; use of simple, traditional tools and farming methods.\n\nOften involves a diversity of crops and livestock (polyculture) to meet family needs and reduce risk.\n\nLow yields per unit area or per unit labor.\n\nHighly dependent on natural conditions (weather, soil fertility).\n\nFocus on food security for the household.\n\nExamples: Shifting cultivation (slash-and-burn) in some tropical regions, nomadic herding, smallholder farming in many developing countries where farmers grow food primarily for themselves.\n\nCommercial Farming (Market-Oriented Farming):\n\nPrimary Purpose: To produce crops and livestock primarily for sale in the market to generate profit.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nLarge scale of operation (large landholdings).\n\nHigh capital investment in land, machinery, technology, and inputs (fertilizers, pesticides, improved seeds/breeds).\n\nHigh degree of mechanization and use of advanced technologies.\n\nOften specializes in a single crop or a few high-value crops/livestock (monoculture or specialized production).\n\nAims for high yields per unit area and high labor productivity.\n\nRelies on hired labor.\n\nMarket-driven: Production decisions are based on market demand and prices.\n\nOften integrated into national and international food supply chains.\n\nExamples: Large-scale wheat farming in North America, plantation agriculture (e.g., rubber, tea, coffee, oil palm), large commercial livestock ranches, intensive poultry or pig operations.\n\nMixed Farming:\n\nAn agricultural system that combines both crop production and livestock raising on the same farm in a complementary way.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nIntegration: Crops may be grown partly to feed livestock, and animal manure can be used to fertilize crops, creating a more integrated and potentially sustainable system.\n\nDiversification of income sources, reducing risk compared to specialized monoculture.\n\nCan be practiced at various scales, from smallholder mixed farms (common in many parts of Africa and Asia) to larger commercial mixed farms.\n\nHelps in efficient use of farm resources and by-products.\n\nCan contribute to soil fertility maintenance.\n\nExample: A farm growing maize and beans, and also raising cattle and chickens. Maize stover can feed cattle, manure fertilizes fields for beans.\n\nOther Classifications/Types (can overlap):\n\nExtensive Farming: Uses large areas of land with low inputs of labor, capital, and resources per unit area (e.g., large-scale cattle ranching on rangelands, traditional shifting cultivation).\n\nIntensive Farming: Uses small areas of land with high inputs of labor, capital, and resources (fertilizers, pesticides, technology) per unit area to maximize yield (e.g., market gardening, greenhouse cultivation, intensive livestock operations).\n\nOrganic Farming: A system that avoids or largely excludes the use of synthetic fertilizers, pesticides, growth regulators, and genetically modified organisms. Relies on crop rotation, green manures, compost, biological pest control.\n\nPlantation Agriculture: Large-scale commercial farming, usually in tropical or subtropical regions, specializing in a single cash crop (e.g., tea, coffee, rubber, sugarcane, bananas, oil palm), often for export.\n\nArable Farming: Focuses on growing crops that are cultivated in fields (e.g., cereals, legumes, root crops).\n\nPastoral Farming (Livestock Ranching): Focuses on raising livestock (cattle, sheep, goats) on pastures or rangelands.\n\nThe type of agricultural system practiced in a region is influenced by factors like climate, soil, topography, socio-economic conditions, technology availability, market access, and government policies.",
