          "title": "5.4 Pig Production and Management Practices",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/4636980/pexels-photo-4636980.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&dpr=2&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_KxcBOHhK8o",
          "content": "**Pigs (Swine - *Sus scrofa domesticus*):**\nRaised for meat (pork, bacon, ham, sausage), lard, and other by-products.\n*   **Terminology:** Boar (intact adult male), Sow (adult female), Gilt (young female before first farrowing), Barrow (castrated male), Piglet/Shoat (young). Farrowing (giving birth).\n*   **Breeds:** (e.g., Large White, Landrace, Duroc, Hampshire - selected for meat quality, growth rate, litter size).\n\n**Husbandry Practices:**\n1.  **Feeding (Nutrition):**\n    *   Pigs are omnivores, monogastric (single stomach). Efficient converters of feed to meat.\n    *   Rations based on grains (maize, sorghum), protein sources (soybean meal), vitamins, minerals.\n    *   Different rations for different stages: Creep feed (piglets), Starter, Grower, Finisher, Breeder (sows, boars).\n    *   Access to clean water.\n2.  **Housing:**\n    *   Protection from extreme weather, good ventilation, dry and clean conditions.\n    *   Concrete floors are common (easier to clean, durable). Bedding may be used.\n    *   Different pens for different groups: Farrowing pens (for sows and piglets, with creep area for piglets), Weaner pens, Grower/Finisher pens, Boar pens.\n    *   Space requirements vary by age/size.\n3.  **Reproduction Management:**\n    *   Gilts reach puberty around 5-8 months. Sows come into estrus (heat) regularly if not pregnant.\n    *   Breeding: Natural mating or Artificial Insemination (AI).\n    *   Gestation period: ~114 days (3 months, 3 weeks, 3 days).\n    *   Farrowing management: Clean, disinfected farrowing pens, assistance if needed, ensuring piglets get colostrum. Large litter sizes common (8-14+).\n4.  **Piglet Management:**\n    *   Iron injections (prevent anemia).\n    *   Tail docking, teeth clipping (to prevent injury/cannibalism - practices vary, welfare considerations).\n    *   Castration (of males not kept for breeding - reduces boar taint in meat).\n    *   Weaning (separating piglets from sow, typically 3-8 weeks).\n5.  **Health Management:**\n    *   **Common Diseases:** Swine fever (classical and African), Porcine Reproductive and Respiratory Syndrome (PRRS), swine dysentery, erysipelas, internal/external parasites.\n    *   **Prevention:** Vaccination, hygiene, biosecurity, good nutrition, parasite control, all-in/all-out systems.\n    *   Regular observation for signs of illness.\n6.  **Waste Management:** Pig manure needs to be managed properly to prevent pollution. Can be used as fertilizer.",
